  "explanation": {
    "area_or_other_cost": "Adds one 32-bit register (32 flops) compared to the baseline (four registers instead of three). The two 32-bit adders remain. Slight increase in routing/muxes may occur but overall area overhead is small.",
    "functional_boundaries": "Preserves module name, ports, and active-low asynchronous reset. Cycle-by-cycle output sequence is identical: y[n] = a[n-2] + b[n-2] + c[n-1] modulo 2^32. Latency from a/b to y remains two cycles, from c to y remains one cycle. No pipeline stage count change.",
    "bottleneck": "The baseline second adder (sum2 <= sum1 + c) has one registered operand (sum1) and one primary input (c). The combinational path from input c through the 32-bit adder to register sum2 can become critical if c arrives late. The output register y is only a delayed copy of sum2.",
    "rewrite": "Retime c and the a+b path so that the second addition (ab_d2 + c_d1) is between two registers. A dedicated register c_d1 captures c without any preceding logic, and the a+b result is delayed by two registers (ab_d1, ab_d2) to preserve the original two-cycle latency from a/b to y. This removes input c from any combinational path before its first register and moves the second adder to a full register-to-register path.",
    "expected_timing_gain": "Moderate. If the input c arrival was limiting, this eliminates the input-to-register adder path, replacing it with a simple register setup. The critical internal path becomes the register-to-register adder (c_d1/ab_d2 to y), which is better balanced and can support a higher clock frequency under late input conditions."
  },
